   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "import numpy as np\n",
    "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "c1633c62-cb72-47bf-831b-de77f5b5f681",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# Exercise 1"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "093944a6-00f0-4c4f-8cde-189d2f116d98",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "In this exercise we consider interpolating a function $f$ on an interval $[a,b]$ with 5 regularly spaced points, including the end points."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "630ece85-3ac0-4d84-83e0-a8877b5fc855",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## (a)\n",
    "\n",
    "Let $a = 0.2$, $b=0.6$ (so spacing $0.1$ between the points), and let $f(x) = \\cos(x)$.\n",
    "\n",
    "Compute an interpolating polynomial $p(x)$ for $f$ by solving a linear system of equations using the monomial basis. Plot $p(x)$ and $f(x)$ (I suggest using the same plot and so that they are both visible). Also plot the interpolation error. Annotate your plots.\n",
    "\n",
    "Then do the same with $a = 1000 \\cdot \\pi +0.2$ and $b = 1000\\cdot \\pi + 0.6$.\n",
    "\n",
    "Describe briefly any notable differences in the resulting plots.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 2,
   "id": "74cc8836-22a3-476c-9b00-52d37d22bc08",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "Interval 1: [0.2000, 0.6000]\n",
      "Coefficients: [ 9.99980422e-01  2.74315910e-04 -5.01438058e-01  3.43915870e-03\n",
      "  3.83136268e-02]\n",
      "Maximum absolute error: 1.2526445480887105e-07\n",
      "Condition number: 18092.252611342537\n",
      "Interval 2: [3141.7927, 3142.1927]\n",
      "Coefficients: [ 2.95508097e+11 -3.76776176e+08  1.80146838e+05 -3.82811729e+01\n",
      "  3.05051386e-03]\n",
      "Maximum absolute error: 0.00030664996480356255\n",
      "Condition number: 4.5075942961953366e+26\n"
     ]
    },
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "",
      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 1000x600 with 4 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "\n",
    "def f(x):\n",
    "    return np.cos(x)\n",
    "\n",
    "intervals = [\n",
    "    (0.2, 0.6),\n",
    "    (1000 * np.pi + 0.2, 1000 * np.pi + 0.6)\n",
    "]\n",
    "\n",
    "fig, axes = plt.subplots(2, 2, figsize=(10, 6))\n",
    "\n",
    "for i, (a, b) in enumerate(intervals):\n",
    "\n",
    "    #Create 5 interpolation points\n",
    "    x = np.linspace(a, b, 5)\n",
    "    y = f(x)\n",
    "\n",
    "    #Construct the Vandermonde matrix and solve it\n",
    "    Vandermonde = np.vander(x, N=5, increasing=True)\n",
    "    coefficients = np.linalg.solve(Vandermonde, y)\n",
    "\n",
    "    x_plot = np.linspace(a, b, 400)\n",
    "    poly_values = np.polynomial.polynomial.polyval(x_plot, coefficients)\n",
    "\n",
    "    #Compute error\n",
    "    error = f(x_plot) - poly_values\n",
    "\n",
    "    axes[i, 0].plot(x_plot, f(x_plot), label=\"cos(x)\")\n",
    "    axes[i, 0].plot(x_plot, poly_values, \"--\", label=\"p(x)\")\n",
    "    axes[i, 0].scatter(x, y, color=\"red\", label=\"Interpolation points\")\n",
    "    axes[i, 0].set_title(f\"Interpolation: interval {i+1}\")\n",
    "    axes[i, 0].set_xlabel(\"x\")\n",
    "    axes[i, 0].set_ylabel(\"y\")\n",
    "    axes[i, 0].legend()\n",
    "    axes[i, 0].grid(True)\n",
    "\n",
    "    axes[i, 1].plot(x_plot, error)\n",
    "    axes[i, 1].set_title(f\"Interpolation error: interval {i+1}\")\n",
    "    axes[i, 1].set_xlabel(\"x\")\n",
    "    axes[i, 1].set_ylabel(\"f(x) - p(x)\")\n",
    "    axes[i, 1].grid(True)\n",
    "\n",
    "    print(f\"Interval {i+1}: [{a:.4f}, {b:.4f}]\")\n",
    "    print(\"Coefficients:\", coefficients)\n",
    "    print(\"Maximum absolute error:\", np.max(np.abs(error)))\n",
    "    print(\"Condition number:\", np.linalg.cond(Vandermonde))\n",
    "\n",
    "plt.tight_layout()\n",
    "plt.show()\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "801329d5-386a-4af7-a2a4-bc9b3e4cf0ba",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "For the first interval, the interpolating polynomial follows the cosine function very closely. The error is small \n",
    "(around $1.25 \\times 10^{-7}$), and the error graph is smooth.\n",
    "\n",
    "For the second interval, the polynomial still looks close to the cosine function, but the error is much larger \n",
    "(around $3.07 \\times 10^{-4}$). The error graph is also much more irregular.\n",
    "\n",
    "This happens because the Vandermonde matrix has a very large condition number for the second interval, making the calculations more sensitive to rounding errors.\n",
    "\n",
    "Overall, the monomial basis works well for the first interval but is much less numerically stable for the second interval."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "58d5e358-2ff6-4dca-b333-53d67a76b505",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## (b)\n",
    "\n",
    "Solve the problem with $a = 1000 \\cdot \\pi + 0.2$ and $b = 1000 \\cdot \\pi + 0.6$ again, but now using Newton polynomial basis function.\n",
    "\n",
    "Compare with the second example of part (a)."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 3,
   "id": "f309b6b6-05e9-4b16-92fe-4326507768a2",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "",
      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 1200x400 with 2 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    },
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "Newton coefficients: [ 0.98006658 -0.24730089 -0.47727032  0.05707824  0.03831363]\n",
      "Maximum absolute error: 1.2526445469784875e-07\n",
      "Condition number: 8120.262291009358\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "\n",
    "#Interval from part (a)\n",
    "interval_start = 1000 * np.pi + 0.2\n",
    "interval_end = 1000 * np.pi + 0.6\n",
    "\n",
    "x = np.linspace(interval_start, interval_end, 5)\n",
    "y = np.cos(x)\n",
    "n = len(x)\n",
    "\n",
    "N = np.ones((n, n))\n",
    "\n",
    "#calculate the Newton basis polynomials\n",
    "for j in range(1, n):\n",
    "    N[:, j] = N[:, j-1] * (x - x[j-1])\n",
    "\n",
    "#Solve for the Newton coefficients\n",
    "coefficients = np.linalg.solve(N, y)\n",
    "\n",
    "x_plot = np.linspace(interval_start, interval_end, 400)\n",
    "poly_values = np.full_like(x_plot, coefficients[-1])\n",
    "\n",
    "for j in range(n-2, -1, -1):\n",
    "    poly_values = coefficients[j] + (x_plot - x[j]) * poly_values\n",
    "\n",
    "#Calculate interpolation error\n",
    "error = np.cos(x_plot) - poly_values\n",
    "\n",
    "fig, axes = plt.subplots(1, 2, figsize=(12, 4))\n",
    "\n",
    "axes[0].plot(x_plot, np.cos(x_plot), label=\"cos(x)\")\n",
    "axes[0].plot(x_plot, poly_values, \"--\", label=\"Newton polynomial\")\n",
    "axes[0].scatter(x, y, color=\"red\", label=\"Interpolation points\")\n",
    "axes[0].set_title(\"Newton interpolation\")\n",
    "axes[0].set_xlabel(\"x\")\n",
    "axes[0].set_ylabel(\"y\")\n",
    "axes[0].legend()\n",
    "axes[0].grid(True)\n",
    "\n",
    "axes[1].plot(x_plot, error)\n",
    "axes[1].set_title(\"Newton interpolation error\")\n",
    "axes[1].set_xlabel(\"x\")\n",
    "axes[1].set_ylabel(\"f(x) - p(x)\")\n",
    "axes[1].grid(True)\n",
    "\n",
    "plt.tight_layout()\n",
    "plt.show()\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"Newton coefficients:\", coefficients)\n",
    "print(\"Maximum absolute error:\", np.max(np.abs(error)))\n",
    "print(\"Condition number:\", np.linalg.cond(N))\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "0884f525-132c-4a84-a1e8-db32a063790b",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Using the Newton polynomial basis, the interpolating polynomial follows the cosine function very closely. The maximum absolute error is around $1.25 \\times 10^{-7}$, and the error graph is smooth.\n",
    "\n",
    "Compared to the monomial basis in part (a), the error is much smaller (previously around $3.07 \\times 10^{-4}$). The condition number also decreases from approximately $4.51 \\times 10^{26}$ to $8.12 \\times 10^3$.\n",
    "\n",
    "This is because the Newton basis uses differences between interpolation points instead of large powers of $x$, making the calculations less sensitive to rounding errors.\n",
    "\n",
    "Therefore, the Newton basis gives a more accurate and numerically stable result for this interval."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "c8e7a698-c13b-41a2-9434-43e2e591892f",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## (c)\n",
    "\n",
    "Explain what you see in the error graphs. What kind of errors are dominant in each of the error graphs? Explain the size of the dominant errors in each graph. N.B. a quantitative explanation is requested. Depending on the type of error you may want to have a look at the numbers in the computation of $p$ for finding your explanation.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 4,
   "id": "c437f48c-4964-49ae-aca9-0592f0561b50",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "Individual polynomial terms: [ 2.95508097e+11 -1.18382798e+12  1.77843081e+12 -1.18741008e+12\n",
      "  2.97299147e+11]\n",
      "Sum of terms: 0.921142578125\n",
      "Actual cos(x): 0.9210609940029748\n",
      "Machine epsilon: 2.220446049250313e-16\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "#Size of polynomial terms in the second interval\n",
    "interval_start = 1000 * np.pi + 0.2\n",
    "interval_end = 1000 * np.pi + 0.6\n",
    "\n",
    "nodes = np.linspace(interval_start, interval_end, 5)\n",
    "V = np.vander(nodes, N=5, increasing=True)\n",
    "coefficients = np.linalg.solve(V, np.cos(nodes))\n",
    "\n",
    "x = 1000 * np.pi + 0.4\n",
    "terms = coefficients * x**np.arange(5)\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"Individual polynomial terms:\", terms)\n",
    "print(\"Sum of terms:\", np.sum(terms))\n",
    "print(\"Actual cos(x):\", np.cos(x))\n",
    "print(\"Machine epsilon:\", np.finfo(float).eps)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "d6db652b-2758-45e8-94d7-cb0d69b26560",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### First interval — Monomial basis\n",
    "\n",
    "For the first interval, the error graph is smooth and the maximum error is approximately $1.25 \\times 10^{-7}$. Inthis case the dominant error is the interpolation error.\n",
    "\n",
    "The interpolation error formula is as following:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "f(x)-p(x)=\\frac{f^{(5)}(\\xi)}{5!}\\prod_{i=0}^{4}(x-x_i)\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Since $f(x)=\\cos(x)$, we know that $|f^{(5)}(\\xi)|\\leq1$. Using the five interpolation points, we obtain the bound:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "|f(x)-p(x)|\\leq\\frac{3.63\\times10^{-5}}{120}\\approx3.03\\times10^{-7}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "This is close to our observed error and explains its size.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Second interval — Monomial basis\n",
    "\n",
    "For the second interval, the maximum error is much larger, approximately $3.07\\times10^{-4}$, and the error graph is irregular. In this case, rounding errors dominate.\n",
    "\n",
    "The polynomial has very large coefficients, such as $c_0\\approx2.96\\times10^{11}$. When evaluating the polynomial, individual terms can become extremely large. For example, although $c_3\\approx-38.28$ which seems low, the term $c_3x^3$ is approximately $-1.19\\times10^{12}$. These large terms must cancel to produce a result close to 1, which leads to rounding errors.\n",
    "\n",
    "From the code, the sum of the polynomial terms is approximately $0.92114258$, while the actual cosine value is $0.92106099$. This gives an error of approximately $8.16\\times10^{-5}$.\n",
    "\n",
    "Using machine epsilon $\\epsilon\\approx2.22\\times10^{-16}$, we can estimate the rounding-error scale for terms of size $10^{12}$:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "10^{12}\\times2.22\\times10^{-16}\\approx2.22\\times10^{-4}\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Since several large terms are involved, this explains why rounding errors of order $10^{-4}$ are reasonable, which is consistent with the observed maximum error.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Third interval — Newton basis\n",
    "\n",
    "With the Newton basis, the maximum error decreases to approximately $1.25\\times10^{-7}$, and the error graph becomes smooth again.\n",
    "\n",
    "The observed error is again close to the theoretical interpolation error bound of $3.03\\times10^{-7}$, so interpolation error is dominant."
   ]
  },
