    "h_values = df[\"h\"].to_numpy()\n",
    "abs_errors = df[\"Absolute Error\"].to_numpy()\n",
    "\n",
    "q = 3\n",
    "\n",
    "plt.loglog(h_values, abs_errors, \"o-\", label=\"Measured Error\")\n",
    "\n",
    "C = abs_errors[-1] / h_values[-1]**q # using the last error to estimate the constant C\n",
    "reference = C * h_values**q\n",
    "\n",
    "plt.loglog(h_values, reference, \"--\", label=r\"Reference for q=3\")\n",
    "\n",
    "plt.xlabel(\"h\")\n",
    "plt.ylabel(\"Absolute Error\")\n",
    "plt.title(\"Power Law $Ch^q$ in Approximation Errors\")\n",
    "plt.legend()\n",
    "plt.show()\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "q_estimated = np.log2(abs_errors[:-1] / abs_errors[1:])\n",
    "print(q_estimated)\n",
    "print(\"Dividing successive errors we can see that as h approaches 0, estimated value of q approaches 3.\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "c2149a44-58e7-4082-b0f2-a8bd6c16f11d",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Power law in h indicates $E(h) = Ch^q$ where $E(h)$ is the absolute error of the approximation, $h$ is spacing between interpolation points, $C$ is a constant and $q$ is the power describing the order of accuracy.\n",
    "\n",
    "The error approximately behaves as $E(h) \\approx Ch^q$ and when we halve $h$ we get $E(\\frac{h}{2}) \\approx C(\\frac{h}{2})^q$. To find how much the error changes as we halve $h$ we divide the two errors $\\frac{E(h)}{E(h/2)} \\approx \\frac{Ch^q}{C(h/2)^q}=2^q$. Taking the base 2 logarithm of both sides gives $q \\approx \\log_2\\left(\\frac{E(h)}{E(h/2)}\\right)$. This is why we can use the successive absolute errors to see what value $q$ approaches to as in the line ```q_estimated = np.log2(abs_errors[:-1] / abs_errors[1:])```. We can just take the floor integer value of the last value. An alternative way to think about this is to think of the regular slope formula between two points for $log(h)$ and $log(E(h))$ which gives the same value $q=3$. \n",
    "\n",
    "To explain why $q$ corresponds to the slope, we take the logarithm of both sides of $E(h)$ and we get $logE(h) = log(Ch^q)$ and using logarithm laws we get $logE(h) = log(C) + qlog(h)$.\n",
    "\n",
    "Comparing this to the equation of a straight line $y = b + mx$ we can see that the slope $m$ corresponds to q. So the slope of the line on a log-log plot gives us the power. \n",
    "\n",
    "Yes the errors satisfy a power law $Ch^q$ in h for $q=3$ as we can see from the reference line. The $C$ in the reference line was found from the last absolute error value as such: $C = \\frac{\\text{Absolute error of last element}}{(\\text{Last used h})^q}$. \n",
    "\n",
    "In standard Lagrange interpolation, with $n$ distinct data points we can construct a unique interpolating polynomial of degree $n-1$ exactly. Since the function $e^x$ is infinitely differentiable, the interpolation error is given by below equation where $\\theta$ is some point within the interpolation interval $0 < \\theta < 3h$.\n",
    "$$  \n",
    "f(x)-p_{n-1}(x)=\\frac{f^{(n)}(\\theta)}{n!}\\prod_{i=0}^{n-1}(x-x_i)  \n",
    "$$ \n",
    "\n",
    "We have four interpolation points so $n=4$ and the points are 0 ,h, 2h, 3h Evaluating at $x=h/2$, we get\n",
    "\n",
    "$$  \n",
    "f(h/2)-p_3(h/2)=\\frac{f^{(4)}(\\theta)}{4!}  \n",
    "\\left(\\frac h2\\right)  \n",
    "\\left(-\\frac h2\\right)  \n",
    "\\left(-\\frac{3h}{2}\\right)  \n",
    "\\left(-\\frac{5h}{2}\\right).  \n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "Simplfying and taking the nth derivative and plugging in $\\theta$ we get:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$  \n",
    "f(h/2)-p_3(h/2)=-\\frac{5}{128}e^\\theta h^4.  \n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "As $h$ approaches zero, $\\theta$ also approaches zero, so $e^\\theta$ approaches 1. Therefore, the absolute error is approximately\n",
    "\n",
    "$$  \n",
    "E(h)\\approx\\frac{5}{128}h^4.  \n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "We can express the error order using Big-O notation as\n",
    "\n",
    "$$  \n",
    "E(h)=O(h^4).  \n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "This means the error is bounded by a constant times $h^4$ for sufficiently small $h$. The error here also approximately follows a power law $Ch^q$, but with $q=4$ instead of $q=3$.\n"
   ]
